released to celebrate the 20th  anniversary of Nintendo's original  Pokemon game Pokemon Go has become an  instant sensation more than 15 million  people downloaded the free game in its  first week with more active daily users  than Twitter Facebook and snapchat let's  look behind what's already one of the  most popular smartphone games around  Google Earth origins Pokemon go combines  online gaming with GPS linked map  technology and both of those  technologies were pioneered by this same  man back in 1996 21 year old John Hanke  created Meridian 59 the first massively  multiplayer online game where gamers  could connect globally over the internet  to explore the game's world and compete  against one another in 2000 Hankey  developed keyhole a system linking roads  and highways with aerial photography to  create the first ever GPS linked 3d map  of the world Hankey later sold keyhole  to Google where it became Google Earth  Hankey went on to spearhead Google Maps  and Google Street View inspired by a  joke each April Fool's Day Google pulls  light-hearted pranks on its users in  2014 they announced the Google Maps  Pokemon challenge we prepared the most  rigorous test known to man to find the  world's best Pokemon master  teaming up with the Pokemon company and  Nintendo the program was a fake game  that supposedly allowed users to capture  Pokemon creatures hidden in the real  world that they could find with Google  Maps so many people desperately wanted  it to be real that the prank went viral  and just two years later the basic  concept became Pokemon go ingress - with  funding from Google hanky launched  Niantic labs in 2010 to add a gaming  layer to maps there he created a game  called ingress the first MMO that  involved the use of gamers real-world  locations it was basically Pokemon go  except without the Pokemon the most  popular user-generated meeting points in  ingress later became the poki stops and  gyms in Pokemon go Pokemon go became  available for Android and iPhone users  in North America Australia and New  Zealand on July 6 2016 it hit Europe and  most of Asia on July 15 that nine-day  gap was too much for some who downloaded  the bootleg or knockoff versions of  Pokemon go in fact at least 43 separate  fake versions of Pokemon go have been  spotted many of which are malicious apps  that flood uses phones with ads and  malware worse some of these bootlegs  could take control of the users phone  without their knowledge mining their  personal data the Hunter has become the  hunted  been in saudi-arabia Pokemon go was  gaining popularity in the Middle East  and clerics in Saudi Arabia are  skeptical to say the least  they've issued a fatwa or Islamic  religious ban on the game which follows  up a previous fatwa against the original  Pokemon games from 1996 according to  them the games trading card aspect  encouraged blasphemy and gambling while  Pokemon go might not use cards it does  apparently contain forbidden images like  symbols that could be mistaken for  Masonic symbolism in other words  Jigglypuff is the devil's  get up and move or diet while the game  helps people get more exercise as they  walk around searching for Pokemon  some still prefer their video games to  be less dangerous actually two people in  San Diego fell off a cliff while playing  the game crafty entrepreneurs in cities  like New York San Francisco and Portland  have placed ads on Craigslist offering  to drive players around while they pokey  hunt some drivers even promised to  provide the one thing coveted by players  even more than a legendary new foreign  charges thanks for watching  subscribe to our YouTube channel to  watch more videos like the one you just  saw and leave us a comment to let us  know which Pokemon you've caught today
 

